	Everything compiles, links and minimally runs (test10k.ctl) on
	sun4 (sampson) PAROS=seq, swampi, lsv
	sun5 (lux)     PAROS=seq, swampi
	i686 (noisy)   PAROS=seq, swampi, lsv
	i686 (naegling) PAROS=swampi (11 processors)

	No other combinations were tested.  Nothing is known to be broken.

	* relerr/main_nv.c: I've been burned once too many times by
	the strncmp(name, "test", 4) test to decide whether to create
	a random data set.  Now changed to test whether the datafile string
	is non-empty in the .ctl file, and if not, it falls back to
	the RNG.  This required changes to the relerr/test*.ctl files.  Also
	changed relerr/main_*.c

	* libsw/swampi.c: Use select instead of sleep when waiting for
 	connections.

	Change the type of ptr and buf to char* so pointer-arithmetic
	is legal on non-GNU compilers.

	A few #ifndefs around non-portable socket manipulations.
	
	Protect the hwclock stuff with #ifdefs.

	Read bogomips from /proc to get clock speed.  This isn't
	necessarily right either, but it's better than using 200.e6.
	
